<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mingyi Nyo</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1459">1459</a>–<a href="/years/1530">1530</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toungoo-dynasty">Toungoo dynasty</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Mingyi Nyo was the founder of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toungoo-dynasty">Toungoo dynasty</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burma">Burma</a>, ruler of the small principality of Toungoo on the Sittaung river from 1485. As the kingdom of Ava weakened, he declared independence in 1510 and built Toungoo into a refuge for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bamar-people">Burmans</a> fleeing Shan raids in the north. His son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tabinshwehti">Tabinshwehti</a> and Tabinshwehti's brother-in-law <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bayinnaung">Bayinnaung</a> built on this base to create the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-toungoo-empire">First Toungoo Empire</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mingyi-nyo.jpg" alt="Mingyi Nyo" width="207" height="242" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Tawoo / Tawoo at en.wikipedia · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mingyi_Nyo.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Mingyi Nyo was born in 1459 to Maha Thinkhaya, a descendant of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyawswa-i-of-pinya">Kyawswa I of Pinya</a>, and Min Hla Nyet, daughter of Sithu Kyawhtin, most likely at Ava, where his grandfather served at the court of Thihathura I until he became viceroy of Toungoo in 1470. After Sithu Kyawhtin's death in 1481 the viceroyship passed to his son Min Sithu, who repeatedly refused Nyo's requests to marry his cousin Soe Min Hteik-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tin">Tin</a>. Nyo murdered his uncle, married his cousin and seized power around April 1485.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>King Minkhaung II of Ava, facing his brother Minye Kyawswa's rebellion at Yamethin, accepted a gift of two young elephants and recognised Nyo as governor, and Toungoo fought for Ava for the rest of the 1490s. Nyo built the fortified city of Dwayawaddy on 11 November 1491. A raid into Hanthawaddy without Ava's leave, in which he killed the Shan governor of Kaungbya in single combat, brought a Hanthawaddy force of 16,000 that besieged Dwayawaddy in late 1495; Toungoo barely survived, and Nyo never again fought his southern neighbour.</p><p>After Minkhaung II died in April 1501, Nyo sheltered those who had tried to kill the new king, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shwenankyawshin">Shwenankyawshin</a>, who sought his loyalty in 1502 with a royal bride and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyaukse">Kyaukse</a> granary. Nyo took Kyaukse, moved many of its people to his capital and joined the rebel princes of Nyaungyan and Prome. In 1510 he founded Ketumati, the present Toungoo, and he was crowned king on 11 April 1511. Ava besieged Toungoo for a month in 1525 without success. When the Confederation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shan-state">Shan States</a> took Ava in March 1527, he laid waste the land between them to make it impassable.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>He died on 24 November 1530 and was succeeded by his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tabinshwehti">Tabinshwehti</a>. Cut off from the Irrawaddy valley between the Bago Yoma and the Karen hill country, Toungoo had been one of the few stable states of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upper-burma">Upper Burma</a>, and the refugees it drew gave Tabinshwehti and his deputy <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bayinnaung">Bayinnaung</a> the manpower to imagine offensive war against larger kingdoms.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Mingyi Nyo” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Toungoo, a small Burmese principality on the Sittaung river, grew in strength under <b>Mingyi Nyo</b>, who declared independence from Ava in 1510.</blockquote><a href="/state/first-toungoo-empire">First Toungoo Empire</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Inwa, in what is now <a href="/countries/myanmar">Myanmar</a>. Died in Taungoo, in what is now <a href="/countries/myanmar">Myanmar</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=21.86,95.99&amp;year=1505" aria-label="Inwa on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Mingyi Nyo"><image href="/maps/globes/g090n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5474 5682h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5474 5682h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=21.86,95.99&amp;year=1505">What was here in 1505? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
